module std where

-- =========================================================================
-- String ⇄ List Char bridges (representation-substrate Phase 3).
--
-- `String` is `$opaque { name:"String", pure: List Char }`: in production the
-- runtime stores a JS string (the repr); the pure value is a `List Char`. These
-- two bridges cross that boundary — they ARE the `Reprable JsRuntime String`
-- lift/lower the design (R5) identified (fenel/std/dispatch-instances.fenel).
--
--   stringToCharList : String -> List Char   the LOWER — split the JS string
--                                             into a cons-spine of Char (the
--                                             REASONING direction).
--   charListToString : List Char -> String   the LIFT  — fold the Char-list back
--                                             into a JS string (the PERF
--                                             direction).
--
-- THE ASYMMETRY vs List (§2e): String's native is a FLAT code-point sequence (a
-- JS string), NOT a container of already-repr'd elements — so its lift/lower DO
-- touch the elements (split into / rejoin chars), whereas List's spine bridge is
-- element-transparent (the array already holds the elements). This is the honest
-- per-(target,type) difference (R4), not an inconsistency.
--
-- THE CHAR SEAM (no charCode needed at this rung): Char's RUNTIME REPR is a
-- one-character String (`Char = $opaque { repr:"codepoint", pure: Nat ∈
-- unicode-scalars }`, surfaced as a 1-char string at the boundary — see
-- type-native.fenel `charCode`). So `chars` (split by Unicode code point — emoji
-- and non-BMP stay grouped) already yields the Char element repr directly, and
-- `join … ""` rejoins them; the codepoint↔Nat crossing via `charCode`/
-- `charFromCode` is Char's OWN `Reprable` (representation-substrate Phase 4), NOT
-- the String rung. So the String bridge REUSES the List spine bridge composed
-- with chars/join — String genuinely IS "List Char with a different native":
-- the List bridge does the cons-spine ↔ array, chars/join do the array ↔ flat
-- string.
--
-- The std-ffi.ts string natives (concat/length/slice/chars/join/… over the
-- "string" repr) are the ledgered opaque-repr PERF BRANCH of this instance
-- (design/ts-exceptions-ledger.md B4 — the canonical "String.concat over
-- List<Char>" class). These bridges are the law/reasoning side.

@label "String to char list"
@description "Split a JS string into a pure cons-spine List of Char (the Reprable JsRuntime String LOWER). stringToCharList = arrayToList ∘ chars: `chars` splits by Unicode code point (emoji/non-BMP stay grouped, each element a 1-char String = Char's runtime repr), then the List lower folds the array into the cons-spine. Reuses the List spine bridge — String is List Char with a flat-string native."
@tags "string" "char" "list" "repr" "bridge"
stringToCharList : (s: String) -> List Char
stringToCharList s = arrayToList (chars s)

@label "Char list to string"
@description "Fold a pure cons-spine List of Char back into a JS string (the Reprable JsRuntime String LIFT). charListToString = join (listToArray cs) \"\": the List lift folds the cons-spine into an array of 1-char Strings (Char's runtime repr), then `join … \"\"` concatenates. The inverse of stringToCharList over the BMP + grouped-codepoint domain `chars` defines."
@tags "string" "char" "list" "repr" "bridge"
charListToString : (cs: List Char) -> String
charListToString cs = join (listToArray cs) ""
